4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Caruana1,c_1axyo1v,ai,True,False,True,Caruana,Supported,high,background,False,False,3,Caruana and his collaborators have shown that it is possible to compress the knowledge in an ensemble into a single model which is much easier to deploy and we develop this approach further using a different compression technique.,True,,"In this paper we show how to compress the function that is learned by a complex model into a much smaller, faster model that has comparable performance. ||| We present a method for model compression that is able to train fast, compact models to mimic better performing, but slow and complex models with little loss in performance. ||| Results on eight test problems show that, on average, the loss in performance due to compression is usually negligible, yet the mimic neural nets are 1000 times smaller and 1000 times faster."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Caruana3,c_zutn7g,ai,True,False,True,Caruana,Supported,medium,background,False,False,3,A version of this strategy has already been pioneered by Rich Caruana and his collaborators.,False,This referenced paper is probably the previous pioneering work that pioneered transferring knowledge from a cumbersome model to a smaller model through distillation.,"The main idea behind model compression is to use a fast and compact model to approximate the function learned by a slower, larger, but better performing model. ||| In this paper we show how to compress the function that is learned by a complex model into a much smaller, faster model that has comparable performance. ||| Compression works by labeling a large unlabeled data set with the target model, and then training a neural net using the newly labeled data."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Caruana4,c_c69miw,ai,True,False,True,Caruana,Supported,medium,background,False,False,3,The transfer set that is used to train the small model could consist entirely of unlabeled data or we could use the original training set.,True,,"Instead of training the neural net on the original (often small) training set used to train the ensemble, we use the ensemble to label a large unlabeled data set and then train the neural net on this much larger, ensemble labeled, data set. ||| In some domains large amounts of unlabeled data is easy to collect (e.g. in text, web and image domains) and can be used as pseudo data. ||| Compression works by labeling a large unlabeled data set with the target model, and then training a neural net using the newly labeled data."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Dietterich20000,c_14xcvf0,ai,True,False,True,Dietterich2000,Partially Supported,low,causal,False,True,2,A very simple way to improve the performance of almost any machine learning algorithm is to train many different models on the same data and then to average their predictions.,True,,This paper has provided a brief survey of methods for constructing ensembles and reviewed the three fundamental reasons why ensemble methods are able to outperform any single classifier within the ensemble. ||| Figure 5 shows the results when training on a training set of size 20. The plot confirms our explanation. The Aggressive AdaBoost initially has much higher error rates on the test set than Standard AdaBoost. It then gradually improves.
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Jacobs10,c_rac3uo,ai,True,False,True,Jacobs,NEI,low,background,False,,3,The use of specialists that are trained on subsets of the data has some resemblance to mixtures of experts which use a gating network to compute the probability of assigning each example to each expert.,False,The use of specialist AI trained on only partial of the data has similarity to the mixtures of experts architecture which use a gating network to compute the probability of assigning each example to each expert.,"We present a new supervised learning procedure for systems composed of many separate networks, each of which learns to handle a subset of the complete set of training cases. ||| If we know in advance that a set of training cases may be naturally divided into subsets that correspond to distinct subtasks, interference can be reduced by using a system composed of several different 'expert' networks plus a gating network that decides which of the experts should be used for each training case. ||| the gating network to alter the responsibilities that get assigned to the experts"
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,Kriz8,c_1awpor8,ai,True,False,True,Kriz,NEI,low,background,False,,0,"When we did this work, Google's baseline model for JFT was a deep convolutional neural network that had been trained for about six months using asynchronous stochastic gradient descent on a large number of cores.",True,,
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,LiZhaoHuangGong7,c_16ie4l4,ai,True,False,True,LiZhaoHuangGong,Supported,low,background,False,False,3,We have recently become aware of related work on learning a small acoustic model by matching the class probabilities of an already trained larger model.,True,,"In this paper, we propose to directly minimize the KL divergence between the output distribution of the small-size DNN and the large-size DNN by leveraging large amounts of un-transcribed data to get a better small-size DNN than using the standard training method with only transcribed data. ||| Then, we use the steps described in Section 3.1 to learn small-size DNNs with 375 hours (hr), 750 hours, and 1500 hours of un-transcribed data from the same Windows Phone short message dictation task. ||| Currently, with 1500 hours of untranscribed data, the small-size DNN learned with our proposed method can get relative 5.08% WER reduction from its counterpart trained with the standard process in Section 2."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,SPM6,c_19g068x,ai,True,False,True,SPM,Supported,low,background,False,False,4,State-of-the-art ASR systems currently use DNNs to map a (short) temporal context of features derived from the waveform to a probability distribution over the discrete states of a Hidden Markov Model (HMM).,True,,"An alternative way to evaluate the fit is to use a feedforward neural network that takes several frames of coefficients as input and produces posterior probabilities over HMM states as output. ||| We have described how three major speech research groups achieved significant improvements in a variety of state-of-the-art ASR systems by replacing GMMs with DNNs, and we believe that there is the potential for considerable further improvement. ||| Finally, a pre-trained DBN-DNN is created by adding a “softmax” output layer that contains one unit for each possible state of each HMM. ||| DBN-DNNs achieved less than half the error rate of shallow neural nets with a single hidden layer. DNN architectures with 5 to 7 hidden layers and up to 2048 hidden units per layer were explored, producing greater than 90% frame-level accuracy for all 21 attributes tested in the full DNN system. On the same data, DBN-DNNs also achieved a very high per frame phone classification accuracy of 86.6%."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,brain-stuff9,c_84viez,ai,True,False,True,brain-stuff,Partially Supported,low,background,False,False,2,This training used two types of parallelism.,True,,"In this section we describe this second level of parallelism, where we employ a set of DistBelief model instances, or replicas, to simultaneously solve a single optimization problem. ||| We have developed a software framework called DistBelief that enables model parallelism within a machine (via multithreading) and across machines (via message passing), with the details of parallelism, synchronization and communication managed by the framework."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,dropout5,c_ol9y0m,ai,True,False,True,dropout,Supported,medium,other,False,False,3,The net was strongly regularized using dropout and weight-constraints as described in.,True,,"Overfitting can be reduced by using “dropout” to prevent complex co-adaptations on the training data. ||| Instead of penalizing the squared length (L2 norm) of the whole weight vector, we set an upper bound on the L2 norm of the incoming weight vector for each individual hidden unit. ||| On each presentation of each training case, each hidden unit is randomly omitted from the network with a probability of 0.5, so a hidden unit cannot rely on other hidden units being present."
4a609692681946af9e14fba66b911683,a3e0239a18b34eef81caf6c53df50529,srivastava2014dropout2,c_1kxwxxl,ai,True,False,True,srivastava2014dropout,Supported,medium,other,False,False,4,The cumbersome model could be an ensemble of separately trained models or a single very large model trained with a very strong regularizer such as dropout.,True,,Dropout is a technique for addressing this problem. ||| The key idea is to randomly drop units (along with their connections) from the neural network during training. ||| Related Work Dropout can be interpreted as a way of regularizing a neural network by adding noise to its hidden units. ||| Conclusion Dropout is a technique for improving neural networks by reducing overfitting.
668e0024b0814d9181768736da410902,8991236a1afc41928d5e2e144f0e2a43,baevski2020wav2vec20,c_x42mgo,ai,True,False,True,baevski2020wav2vec2,Supported,high,background,False,False,1,"Although self-supervised and weakly supervised pretraining has improved general ASR performance (e.g., wav2vec~2.0~ and Whisper~), domain mismatch remains a major source of error, motivating the need for recent, domain-specific speech resources.",True,,"wav2vec 2.0: A Framework for Self-Supervised Learning of Speech Representations {abaevski,henryzhou7,abdo,michaelauli}@fb.com Facebook AI Abstract We show for the first time that learning powerful representations from speech audio alone followed by fine-tuning on transcribed speech can outperform the best semi-supervised methods while being conceptually simpler. wav2vec 2.0 masks the speech input in the latent space and solves a contrastive task defined over a quantization of the latent representations which are jointly learned."
