a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,radford2019language5,c_1ywbixy,ai,True,False,True,radford2019language,Partially Supported,low,background,False,False,1,"The model proposed here is built from several components which include (a) 876k Wikipedia sentences, addressible via embeddings; (b) a pretrained GPT-2-117M language model which was noted to be incapable of answering questions successfully in; and (c) a scheme for incorporating `sentence hints' into the language generation context.",True,,"GPT-2 answers 4.1% of questions correctly when evaluated by the exact match metric commonly used on reading comprehension datasets like SQUAD.3 As a comparison point, the smallest model does not exceed the 1.0% accuracy of an incredibly simple baseline which returns the most common answer for each question type (who, what, where, etc...)."
a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,rajpurkar2016squad0,c_1fl16p8,ai,True,False,True,rajpurkar2016squad,Supported,high,other,False,False,2,The field of question answering has been dominated by supervised methods for competitive tasks such as the Stanford question answering dataset (SQuAD).,True,,"To address the need for a large and high-quality reading comprehension dataset, we present the Stan- ford Question Answering Dataset v1.0 (SQuAD), freely available at https://stanford-qa.com, consisting of questions posed by crowdworkers on a set of Wikipedia articles, where the answer to every question is a segment of text, or span, from the corresponding reading passage. ||| We present the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), a new reading comprehension dataset consisting of 100,000+ questions posed by crowdworkers on a set of Wikipedia articles, where the answer to each question is a segment of text from the corresponding reading passage. We analyze the dataset to understand the types of reasoning required to answer the questions, leaning heavily on dependency and constituency trees. We build a strong logistic regression model, which achieves an F1 score of 51.0%, a significant improvement over a simple baseline (20%). However, human performance (86.8%) is much higher, indicating that the dataset presents a good challenge problem for future research. The dataset is freely available at https://stanford-qa.com."
a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,snli:emnlp20159,c_6bn99h,ai,True,False,True,snli:emnlp2015,Supported,medium,other,False,False,1,"paper, but USE is not a purely unsupervised model: ``We augment unsupervised learning with training on supervised data from the Stanford Natural Language Inference (SNLI) corpus''.",True,,"To address this, this paper introduces the Stanford Natural Language Inference (SNLI) corpus, a collection of sentence pairs labeled for entailment, contradiction, and semantic independence."
a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,sutskever-openai-video16,c_143vd55,ai,False,False,True,,,,,,,0,,,,
