a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,yogatama2019learning1,c_3loac6,ai,True,False,True,yogatama2019learning,Supported,medium,causal,True,False,3,"However, as discussed in, some of these datasets are becoming over-optimised for, making the architectures less generally applicable.",True,,"This state of affairs promotes development of models that only work well for a specific purpose, overestimates our success at having solved the general task, fails to reward sample efficient generalization that requires the ability to discover and make use of rich linguistic structures, and ultimately limits progress. ||| Our results indicate that models that work well on SQuAD still require training examples from other datasets before it can serve as a general-purpose question answering model. ||| Moreover, we find that far from solving general tasks (e.g., document question answering), our models are overfitting to the quirks of particular datasets (e.g., SQuAD)."
f21df9ba94974b7ab657f229ef157fc2,f7c16d6748f5474499aa37b36b3d31cf,2d11,c_1sklwko,ai,True,False,True,2d,Supported,high,other,False,False,1,"We can evaluate SWEs' understanding of their artifacts using other assessment techniques, including other parsons puzzles variations (Adaptive~, Faded~, and Two-Dimensional~) and fault injection as discussed earlier, but they all rely on us having located segments of the artifacts against which to evaluate the SWEs' understanding, as we cannot expect them to reconstruct entire artifacts.",True,,We introduce a novel family of Parson’s puzzles where the lines of code need to be sorted in two dimensions.
